From: usb-proxy appliance <usb-proxy@local>
Subject: [PATCH] dts: orangepi-zero: cap CPU at 816 MHz, add ramoops crash log

Two appliance-resilience changes against intermittent oopses with corrupt
pointers (random register/stack corruption, worst at boot):

 - Delete the 1008 MHz OPP. vdd-cpux is a two-state GPIO regulator
   (1.1 V / 1.3 V on PL6) and only the 1008 MHz OPP needs the 1.3 V state.
   Measured on the appliance: schedutil flaps 648<->1008 many times per
   second under mere shell activity, toggling the rail with it (4 ms
   RC-network ramp) - that switching transient is the prime suspect for
   the corruption (the DRAM 624->480 downclock in U-Boot reduced but did
   not eliminate it). With 1008 gone both remaining OPPs run at 1.1 V and
   the rail never switches. The proxy is USB-latency-bound and idles at
   648 MHz, so this costs nothing.

 - Reserve 128 KiB at 252 MiB for ramoops/pstore (CONFIG_PSTORE_RAM in
   usbproxy-resilience.cfg). The rootfs is a RAM initramfs and the kernel
   now reboots itself on any oops/panic, so this is the only place the
   crash text survives for post-mortem reading (/sys/fs/pstore).

Signed-off-by: usb-proxy appliance <usb-proxy@local>
---
--- a/arch/arm/boot/dts/allwinner/sun8i-h2-plus-orangepi-zero.dts
+++ b/arch/arm/boot/dts/allwinner/sun8i-h2-plus-orangepi-zero.dts
@@ -68,6 +68,26 @@
 		stdout-path = "serial0:115200n8";
 	};
 
+	/* usb-proxy appliance: persistent crash log (ramoops/pstore). The
+	 * rootfs is a RAM initramfs and the kernel reboots itself on any
+	 * oops/panic (panic_on_oops + panic timeout), so without this the
+	 * crash text would be gone before anyone could read it. 128 KiB at
+	 * 252 MiB: inside RAM on the 256 MiB Zero, ~4 MiB below top-of-RAM
+	 * so U-Boot's relocation/stack never scribbles it, far above the
+	 * kernel+initramfs load area. Pair with CONFIG_PSTORE_RAM. */
+	reserved-memory {
+		#address-cells = <1>;
+		#size-cells = <1>;
+		ranges;
+
+		ramoops@4fc00000 {
+			compatible = "ramoops";
+			reg = <0x4fc00000 0x20000>;
+			record-size = <0x8000>;
+			console-size = <0x10000>;
+		};
+	};
+
 	leds {
 		compatible = "gpio-leds";
 
@@ -123,6 +143,20 @@
 	cpu-supply = <&reg_vdd_cpux>;
 };
 
+/* usb-proxy appliance: cap the CPU at 816 MHz. vdd-cpux is a two-state
+ * GPIO regulator (1.1 V / 1.3 V on PL6); only the 1008 MHz OPP needs the
+ * 1.3 V state, so with it gone the rail sits at a constant 1.1 V and
+ * cpufreq never toggles PL6 again. Measured on the appliance: schedutil
+ * flaps 648<->1008 many times per second under mere shell activity,
+ * switching the rail with it (4 ms RC-network ramp) - that transient is
+ * the prime suspect for the intermittent oopses with corrupt pointers
+ * (random register/stack corruption, worst at boot = peak flapping; the
+ * DRAM 624->480 downclock reduced but did not eliminate them). The proxy
+ * is USB-latency-bound and idles at 648 MHz, so 816 costs nothing. */
+&cpu0_opp_table {
+	/delete-node/ opp-1008000000;
+};
+
 &ehci0 {
 	/* OTG host companion (PHY0): unused - the OTG port is the musb
 	 * gadget. Disabling releases PHY0 to musb and trims USB boot time. */
